{{block center|<math>\text{U}_1/\text{U}_2 = \sqrt{\{\gamma_1\rho_2/\gamma_2\rho_1\}}</math>.}}
This formula was very nearly confirmed for hydrogen, carbon dioxide and nitrous oxide.

J. Violle and T. Vautier (''Ann. chim. phys.'', 1890, vol. 19) made observations with a tube 0·7 m. in diameter, and, using Regnault's apparatus, found that the velocity could be represented by
{{block center|<math>331{\cdot}3(1+\text{C}\sqrt{\text{P}})</math>,}}
where <math>\text{P}</math> is the mean excess of pressure above the normal. According to von Helmholtz and Kirchhoff the velocity in a tube should be less than that in free air by a quantity depending on the diameter of the tube, the frequency of the note used, and the viscosity of the gas (Rayleigh, ''Sound'', vol. ii. §§ 347–8).
